177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 646 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables. This means we give a name to a pin number so it is easy to use in the code. For example, if we write int ledPin = 5;, it means we are using pin number 5 and calling it ledPin.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 647 0 R
/E (Next comes the setup\(\) function. This part runs only one time when the Arduino is turned on. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell the Arduino whether a pin will work as an input or an output. For example, setting a pin as OUTPUT allows it to control devices like an LED.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 648 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever, so the Arduino keeps checking or controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 649 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we use digitalWrite\(\) to turn an output ON or OFF. For example, digitalWrite\(ledPin, HIGH\); will turn the LED ON, and digitalWrite\(ledPin, LOW\); will turn it OFF.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 83 0 R
/E (So overall, the program first prepares everything in setup\(\), then keeps running the loop continuously, controlling devices like LEDs using functions such as pinMode\(\) and digitalWrite\(\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
