332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 707 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables to give names to pin numbers. The setup\(\) function runs one time when the Arduino starts and is used to prepare the pins. Inside it, pinMode\(\) tells whether a pin is input or output.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 708 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever, so the Arduino keeps checking or controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [27]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (So overall, setup\(\) prepares the program once, and loop\(\) keeps controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
